const NEWS = [{"t":"ARI","x":"Whatever you may think of the Cardinals’ offense, best to lower expectations this week. Seattle has one of the league’s best defenses, and looked it while holding New England to 277 net yards and 10 points last week. Arizona scored 20-22 points while losing both games a year ago, but that looks like their ceiling here. Tyler Allgeier started and played about 60 percent of the snaps at Los Angeles. Jeremiyah Love played about 40 percent, but scored from 5 yards out and had a 4-2 edge in targets in the passing game. Most importantly, the rookie showed no ill effects from the ankle injury he suffered in the preseason. It looks like a timeshare, but it was encouraging to see Love appear both healthy and a factor around the end zone, while also being the favored receiver, as expected. As for this week, though, it’s a pretty tough matchup. Seattle ranked 3rd in run defense a year ago, allowing a league-low 9 touchdowns, and made Rhamondre Stevenson look bad in Week 1 (18 carries, 51 yards). Operating in a committee won’t help matters. Stevenson caught 5 passes for 44 yards in that game; that and his obvious talent are the best arguments for Love in this matchup. Arizona’s best pass catcher for these games a year ago, Emari Demercado, caught 5 passes and a touchdown. Jacoby Brissett started at Seattle last season, completing only half his passes but for 258 yards and 2 TDs. He had a lot of games like that a year ago, and another in Week 1 (277 yards and a score while upsetting the Chargers). Brissett can play, and he’s got a nice group of weapons around INDEX WEEKLY 16, 2026 him. Best to tread carefully here, with Seattle the 13th-ranked pass defense a year ago, allowing just 20 touchdowns in 17 games (a quarter of which where thrown by the league MVP). Above-average yards are a possibility (Arizona shouldn’t do much on the ground), but just 1 TD seems likely. Brissett had an 11-yard run last week, so he’ll run a little, but not a big part of his game these days. Last season: 12 yards per week, with 1 TD. Those who selected Trey McBride as the top tight end are feeling good this week, with Brock Bowers dealing with a troublesome knee and McBride having one of his typical games at LA (9 for 95 with a touchdown). McBride was solid in this series a year ago: 7 for 52 in the home game, with Kyler Murray at quarterback, and 9 for 127 with a touchdown at Seattle, with Br"},{"t":"ATL","x":"Varnon, of Perland, TX was one of only 15-percent of managers to start Isaiah Likely. The Giants’ break out TE was one of the five top scoring players who contributed to Brack’s massive 253.00 points. This matchup is a wee bit better, with the Panthers allowing 59 points to the Bears last week. The Falcons won’t be doing that; they may not know who their quarterback is, again, until the end of the week. But a better spot for them to perhaps come through with a couple of touchdowns and make a run at 17-20 points. Bijan Robinson accounted for a comically high 66 percent of last week’s offense. it wasn’t entirely surprising; that’s why he was a top2 selection in every draft. Some defenses may do a better job of forcing other elements of this offense to beat them, but little reason to think Carolina will be the ones to pull it off. They see Robinson twice a year, and success has been elusive. In the last four meetings Robinson has averaged 133 total yards and scored 6 touchdowns. Not only will he be far and away the best runner but he’s clearly going to be the best receiver in a lot of games. Arguably the worse the Falcons look at quarterback the better Robinson will be as a receiver (and the worse the other pass catchers will look). When Robinson needs a breather, Brian Robinson will play (31 yards while playing a quarter of the snaps last week), but not an offense that will be supporting a second back any time soon. Reports indicate Michael Penix (knee) is at least another week away from game action, so it will be either Tua Tagovailoa (dealing with an oblique injury) or Cooper Rush at quarterback. (Outside chance it will be undrafted rookie Jack Strand, with Rush reportedly dealing with back spasms.) Rush struggled enough at Pittsburgh to make Tagovailoa or Strand look appealing. The matchup itself looks good, with the Panthers just average against the pass a year ago and then lit up by Caleb Williams last week. But Atlanta, whichever possibility draws the start, looks pretty certain to have a bottom-10 passing game -- at best. The main job will be to get the ball into the hands of their franchise running back and hope he can carry them to a low-scoring victory. Drake London and Kyle Pitts are talents who have overcome lesser quarterbacking in the past, but last week wa"},{"t":"BAL","x":"The Saints are probably above-average defensively; they were last year, anyway. But they were most vulnerable to the run, which was the case in Week 1 (165 yards by the Lions). Looks like a good situation for the Ravens to lead with their bread and butter and come away with above-average numbers and a win. should get a heavy Derrick Henry workload. He was on top of his game in the opener, running for 144 yards and 3 touchdowns while averaging 6.0 per attempt. That won’t happen every week, but a year ago (for what it’s worth) the Colts defense he just tore up was ranked 12 spots higher in run defense than the Saints (19th). New Orleans just got gouged for 156 yards and 2 TDs by Jahmyr Gibbs, and here’s no less of a challenge for them. Henry caught his only target for 19 yards last week; it’s not a big part of his game, but it’s a new offense so maybe those plays will continue to show up on occasion. had a minimal role at Justice Hill Indianapolis, carrying 6 times for 18 yards and catching a 10-yard pass. Those anticipating a bounce-back year from had to Lamar Jackson be pleased with the opener, with Jackson rolling up 324 passing yards, 40 rushing yards and 2 total touchdowns, one a run on a designed play. It seemed notable with Jackson running in only 2 TDs on his own all of last season, while averaging a career-low 27 rushing yards and passing for more than 250 yards just once. This is a tougher matchup, with the Saints 5th in pass defense a year ago and holding Jared Goff to 206 and 2 TDs last week (and with a chunk of that in overtime). New Orleans is more vulnerable to the run, suggesting the quarterback may spend a lot of time handing the ball off. But solid early returns on Jackson. Zay Flowers had a huge opener -- for the second year in a row -even more impressive considering he left early with a hamstring injury. The team is calling him day-to-day, so for now we’re listing him with a conditional, “if active” ranking, but a decent chance he’ll need to miss at least this game. Baltimore also lost Ja’Kobi Lane to a fractured wrist in that game, and it sounds like Lane will miss extended time. The rookie had a short touchdown erased by a holding penalty before departing. Next in line at the position is hazy; it was veteran Chris Moore and returner Lajohntay Wester joining Rashod Bateman last week, but Devontez Walker and Elijah Sarratt were both inactiv"},{"t":"BUF","x":"The Bills just hung 420 yards and 4 touchdowns on what’s been one of the league’s best defenses in recent years, while the Lions just allowed over 500 total yards and 4 touchdowns to the Saints, in Detroit. There was an overtime period in there and both of those shootouts may ultimately turn out to be outliers, but it’s a strong Bills offense playing its home opener in a new stadium in primetime against a visiting defense that was below-average a year ago and may. Not surprisingly, the over-under of 53.5 is the highest of the week. Josh Allen comes off a week-winning day (357 total yards and 4 TDs), more impressive considering it was at a Houston defense that had held in him check in each of the previous two seasons. Detroit shouldn’t be as good, 21st in pass defense a year ago and lit up for 410 and 3 by Tyler Shough last week. There’s talent (5 sacks and 2 interceptions in that game, while making Shough look pretty bad for a half), but not anything that should be slowing Allen down. He was a little fortunate to avoid a late turnover at Houston, but a pretty strong game. A short week seems like the only possible negative (but that will affect both defenses, too). Numbers were poor for James Click the “Your Stuff” is Back! bar, or “Your Products” easy. It’s Free! down menu. Check We hope you will team a nickname Next, choose your - a $25 Fantadrop your picks. week’s top scoring mark will appear for fun and fame. Make a mistake? lineup of 2 QBs, defenses. We use Win this week’s the catch: Each Submit your the board. For out Lamar stars. Cook at Houston, with 69 total yards and no scores. He looked fine (4.4 per attempt), but the passing game was clicking and Houston is tough to run on (4th a year ago). The Lions were also better against the run (14th) than pass (21st) a year ago, and they held the Saints in check last week (91 yards and a score on 25 carries). So a below-average situation, but those with Cook will likely want to start him anyway. He had 16 of the position’s 18 touches last week (13 carries and 3 receptions on 4 targets), and there will probably be plenty of offense again here. DJ Moore turned 8 targets into 5 receptions for 100 yards last"},{"t":"CAR","x":"The Panthers scored more than 30 points in a game just once last year, which is one positive to take from their 59-37 Week 1 loss. That was a Bears defense that was leaky a year ago, but it’s encouraging to see the Panthers have some ability to move the ball. The Falcons just held Pittsburgh to only 13 offensive points, so perhaps they’re better than anticipated, but not a top group. Carolina scored exactly 30 while winning each meeting a year ago. We’re not going that high, but another outing at least in the mid-20s looks reasonable. is essentially playing Bryce Young for a new contract, and he helped himself in the opener. That was a Bears defense outside the top 20 against both the run and the pass a year ago, but Young made all the throws in compiling 361 yards and 3 TDs, adding a heady 7-yard touchdown run (taking a big hit at the end of it). Wind back to last year’s playoff loss to the Rams (when he threw for 264 while throwing a TD and running for another), and he’s starting to look like a respectable option. Atlanta is missing those two first-round pass rushers but seems to have some ability to play pass defense. They were slightly above-average a year ago and largely frustrated Aaron Rodgers last week (24 of 40 for 221 yards and a score). Call it an average group (better than Chicago anyway), but not a daunting matchup. Young’s willingness to run near the goal line is also a plus for his value. He had 4 total TDs (3 passes and a run) in last year’s series. Carolina talked about a Chuba and Hubbard Jonathon Brooks committee in August, and when Hubbard missed time with a hamstring injury, it looked like maybe Brooks would become the leader of this backfield. Maybe that will happen at some point, but it hasn’t yet. Hubbard played about 70 percent of the snaps against Chicago and went 10 for 49 and a score on the ground while also catching 3 passes. Brooks played 20 percent of the time and carried 4 times for 14 yards, with a 48-yard reception. Possible they just like Hubbard more right now, but they’re also carefully easing in a player whose first two seasons were wrecked by injury. In any case, Hubbard is the back to consider, and it looks like a reasonable situation. Atlanta had a bottom-10 run defense a year ago and might not be much better; Rico Dowdle didn’t go anywhere, but Jaylen Warren’s 10 carries went for 46 yards. Hubbard’s chances in the pa"},{"t":"CHI","x":"That was some pounding Ben Johnson’s squad just laid on Carolina: 560 total yards and 59 points. They’ve got some firepower, but they should come back to earth some here. The Vikings have one of the league’s best defenses: top-7 a year ago, even with no help from the offense. They didn’t necessarily look it against Green Bay last week, though they did make some plays at the end. The oddsmakers numbers suggest the Bears will be scoring in the mid20s. The teams split last year’s series (27-24 and 19-17). Caleb Williams looked like an MVP candidate at Carolina. That was a modest defense that hasn’t had any kind of pass rush for years, but Williams did it (269 yards passing and 2 TDs) despite not working with either of his top wideouts for most of August. The rushing numbers (65 yards and 2 TDs) were also very encouraging. The Vikings will be ready for him; they mostly held Williams in check a year ago. He averaged just 202 yards passing, but also 42 rushing, with 2 total touchdowns. This Minnesota defense may not be as good, while Williams himself looks ready to take his game to another level. The Vikings last year ranked 3 outside the top 20 against the run, but contained Green Bay’s ground game pretty easily last week, so more of the offense might be put on Williams’ shoulders. Tougher matchup, especially in terms of pass rush, but no reason to shy away from Williams right now. D’Andre Swift wasted no time paying dividends on his recent contract extension, rolling up 134 total yards and 3 TDs. Just 1 reception, but Chicago was playing from ahead for a lot of the game. He’s their guy, and should be more good games coming up. Kyle Monangai also went for 100 yards and a score, thanks to a 61-yard breakaway. Monangai will be more hit or miss (just 10 carries, and he’s not a big part of the passing game), but it was about a 60-40 snapcount split, so there will be good games when the ground game is working. That looks iffier here; the Vikings have some talent in the front seven and held Green Bay back to 66 yards on 20 carries last week. But this was the 21st-ranked run defense a year ago, so too soon to say they’ve got that cleaned up, and Chicago has a much better ground game. Chicago was held in check in the opener last year (Swift carried 17 times for 53 yards) but was better in the rematch (90 rushing yards for Swift, while Monangai scored). Luther Burden lost"},{"t":"CIN","x":"The Bengals scored 33 points last week, so strange to say their offense was disappointing (4 field goals and 2 defensive touchdowns). So was Houston’s defense, allowing over 400 yards and 4 TDs, granted against a good Bills offense. Some course correction is probably in order. We’re calling it a slightly below-average situation for the Bengals. Houston had one of the best defenses against both the run and the pass a year ago, but got lit up for 334 yards by Josh Allen last week. Joe Burrow is a similarly capable passer, while not offering the same dual-threat dimension that makes the Bills quarterback so hard to defend. The Texans defense is a problem: top-7 in both sacks and takeaways in each of the last two seasons. They’ll be coming after Burrow, and will jump on mistakes. But last week highlighted some coverage flaws, and Burrow’s got the receiving corps that can take advantage. We’re putting him down for only slightly below his usual. He’ll run a little (5 for 20 last week), although probably not a big part of his game. The Texans look sound against the run. They were 4th a year ago, allowing 94 yards per game, and held last year’s rushing champ James Cook in check (57 yards) last week. So a poor situation for Chase Brown, although that’s only part of his game anyway. Brown the past two seasons averages 61 yards rushing and 24 receiving, with 13 touchdown runs and 9 receptions. Week 1 looked similar: 56 and a score on the ground, 5 for 22 as a receiver (tackled inside the 5-yard line on two other series). A lesser matchup, but too big a part of the offense to underestimate. Samaje Perine offers some deep PPR league potential, with 50 total yards (5 runs and 3 receptions) as a change-of-pace last week. Better days await Cincinnati’s other offensive stars. Ja’Marr Chase and Tee Higgins were both banged-up at the end of August, which maybe played into their modest Week 1 output. This is a tough matchup, but Houston’s secondary didn’t trouble DJ Moore and company too much last week. Cincinnati sent 11 targets to tight ends last week, resulting in 9 receptions and a touchdown, so maybe there was something about the matchup they liked. That’s possible again (Buffalo also had success with that position), b"},{"t":"CLE","x":"The Browns showed up in Jacksonville for Week 1 in a literal sense, but the game was essentially over before it began. The team looked in over its head with a 24-0 halftime deficit, and the 34-10 final didn’t do justice to how poorly Cleveland played. Best that can be said is this matchup looks a little better, although the betting lines suggest the Browns will be finishing with about 16 points. While he didn’t play well in Week 1, there’s no sign the Browns will bench veteran quarterback Deshaun Watson yet. Watson padded the stat sheet in the second half and finished 16 of 22 for 205 yards and one score, along with 6 rushes for 38 yards. He was under heavy pressure for much of the game, taking 5 sacks. This week’s opponent picked up where it left off last season on defense, allowing 254 yards to Joe Burrow after finishing 27th at 255 yards per game. The Bucs also lost starting corner Jacob Parrish to a second-half injury and leaned on Benjamin Morrison and Zyon McCollum on the perimeter. If you’re forced to play Watson for some godforsaken reason, the matchup itself looks serviceable. Unfortunately for the Browns running game and Quinshon Judkins, an immediate deficit limited opportunities on the ground. Judkins finished with only 12 rushes for 33 yards and 2 catches for 17, well below last year’s 16 rush attempts per game. The issue this week is a historically strong Bucs run defense, with Vita Vea helping plug the middle, that was 5th-best last season at 99 yards per game and continued to do a strong job in Week 1 (106 yards allowed). With Dylan Sampson going down with a knee injury last week and expected to miss significant time, Raheim Sanders is the next man up behind Judkins, though he’s unlikely to have much value unless Judkins also misses time. Rookies KC Concepcion and Denzel Boston were two of the bright spots last week. As advertised, Concepcion was involved in every way the Browns could manage, with 3 carries for 15 yards and 4 receptions for 43 yards. Boston was the deep threat with 2 catches for 59 yards, including an impressive 46-yard touchdown in garbage time. Boston actually outpaced Concepcion in snaps (47 to 35). After catching 20 touchdowns over his last two seasons at Washington, Boston profiles as a high-upside, feast-or-famine option in deep leagues, while Concepcion’s Deebo Samuel-like role in the offense gives him a much higher fl"},{"t":"DAL","x":"An offense that was elite a year ago looked mortal in Week 1, sputtering to 244 yards at the Giants. Too early to say whether Washington’s defense is also improved, though it was respectable at Philadelphia. Dallas averaged 37 points and scored 8 total touchdowns in last year’s series, so the belief is it’s a strong matchup for Brian Schottenheimer’s team. We’re expecting a home rebound this week, as are the oddsmakers (over-under of 50.5 is the 2nd-highest of the week). Last year an average of 60 points were scored in the two games. Start with Dak Prescott, given that Washington had a bottom-5 pass defense a year ago and allowed 33 touchdown passes (3rd-most). That included Prescott averaging 286 passing yards in the two games, with 5 touchdowns. Jalen Hurts threw for just 203 yards last week, but 3 of his 14 completions went for touchdowns, and that’s not the kind of passing game that this has been lately. Prescott was held to just 175 yards by the Giants, but there were several costly drops, and he did throw 2 TDs. Prescott has long tended to play his best ball at home; last year four of his six 300-yard passing performances were at AT&T stadium, and one of the road qualifiers was against this defense. The Giants did a nice job on CeeDee Lamb and George Pickens last week, holding the duo to a combined 8 for 72, with a Lamb TD. It was not, however, without some help: Pickens dropped a pair, including one that might have gone for a long score, while Lamb also had a drop (and drew an end-zone flag that was admittedly dubious). Washington generally kept Philadelphia’s wideouts in check, but this is a better duo than most teams can offer up, and Washington is a year removed from allowing 17 touchdowns to wide receivers (tied for 7th-most) while allowing this duo to each average 78-80 yards in the series, with a touchdown by Lamb. Too early to say they’ll have much more success covering wide receivers this year. Dallas doesn’t look likely to have a notable No. 3, with Ryan Flournoy catching 2 passes on 4 targets last week. Skeptics of Jake Ferguson note that his best stretch of games last year came with Lamb out with an ankle injury. He had the 3rd-fewest yards among his own team’s tight ends a week ago. But he caught 3 TDs in the two games against Washington last season, and Lamb was available for both those contests. Some bounce-back potential for him here. J"},{"t":"DEN","x":"Sean Payton made a controversial decision in the offseason, turning over play-calling duties to Davis Webb, and that decision is already under the microscope after one game. The Broncos were out of sorts on both sides of the ball in a 31-10 loss at Kansas City on Monday night, and they have to clean it up quickly on a short week against a Jaguars team coming off a 34-10 rout of Cleveland. Getting home should help. Denver is a 2.5-point favorite with an implied total around 23 points, right in line with last season’s 23.6-point average (14th). Bo Nix looked rusty in his first game since an ankle injury ended his 2025 season in the playoffs. He completed only 17 of 28 passes for 131 yards, with 1 touchdown and 1 interception, and fumbled 3 times, finishing with an 8.3 QBR. There’s a lot to clean up between his accuracy and the play-calling, but a return to Mile High could be just what the doctor ordered. Nix went 8-1 at home in last year’s regular season, averaging 252 passing yards with 15 touchdown passes (versus 10 on the road). His most productive passing game of the year also came against Jacksonville in Week 16: 28 of 47 for 352 yards, with 1 touchdown and 1 interception. (Admittedly, some of that was cheap production at the end of a one-sided loss.) The Jaguars held down Deshaun Watson in Week 1, but they allowed 231 passing yards per game last season (19th) and managed just 32 sacks (27th) before their 5-sack performance against the Browns. Smart money is that Week 1 said as much about Cleveland’s offense as Jacksonville’s defense. Expect a rebound from Nix. Playing from behind for most of the opener, the Broncos never found a rhythm on the ground, rushing just 15 times for 61 yards. J.K. Dobbins led the way with 8 carries for 36 yards, while RJ Harvey had 7 touches, including 4 catches for 23 yards. Harvey’s receiving role makes him the slightly safer option. Unfortunately, stopping the run is Jacksonville’s strength. The Jaguars allowed a league-low 86 rushing yards per game last season and just 13 rushing touchdowns (8th-fewest), and Cleveland was right in line with 87 yards in Week 1. Harvey did break a 38yard touchdown run in last year’s meeting, but it wouldn’t be a shock if both backs are stuck in the mud for at least another week. Harvey, for what he’ll do in the passing game, is the back to consider. Denver’s wide receivers combined for only 7"},{"t":"DET","x":"Give the Lions credit for playing to expectations in Week 1, rolling up 371 yards and 4 touchdowns (while giving up even more yards and the same number of touchdowns in an overtime win). This week has the potential for more of the same, with Buffalo also coming off a shootout where both teams scored in the 30s. it’s a short week and maybe players are tired from running up and down the field, but that will affect defenses too. Another good week to start key Lions. Buffalo had a bottom-5 run defense a year ago and allowed a league-high 24 touchdowns on the ground, in the unlikely event anyone needs further encouragement to start Jahmyr Gibbs. He did all anyone could have asked in the opener, running for 154 yards and 2 TDs and catching all 5 passes thrown his way for 30 more. The team even spelled him for a quarter of the snaps, perhaps cognizant of the need to give him a breather at times. Regardless, he’s great and he’s a one-man show (Sione Vaki had 2 carries and 1 target, at least clarifying that he’s the correct backup, since Jacob Saylors only played special teams). Buffalo just let Gibbs’ former teammate David Montgomery score 3 touchdowns. Isiah Pacheco, incidentally, isn’t expected to return from IR before December, so no need to burn that roster spot. Jared Goff will probably be somewhat hit-or-miss. The team will more commonly lean on the run, leaving Goff with some ordinary yardage games and watching his running back score. This isn’t the week to worry too much about that, with Josh Allen on the other sideline, and in fact the Lions will have plenty of games against capable offenses along the way. In any case, C.J. Stroud just threw for 274 and 2 in a shootout with the Bills, and Goff and the Lions are capable of the same or better. Goff won’t help himself as a runner, that’s the main negative to his game. A mostly typical week for AmonRa St. Brown and Jameson Williams against the Saints, for good and ill. St. Brown caught 10 passes and 2 TDs, while Buffalo just let Houston’s alpha Nico Collins put up a 7-75-1 line. Williams saw 9 targets, but only 4 were completed and 2 were drops; this after he dropped 12 balls a year ago. The Lions will live with it -- they don’t have a better option, and Williams does plenty of great things too -and he’ll probably get a few shots to bounce back in what should be a high-scoring game. Those were the only wideout"},{"t":"GB","x":"If the 39-22 loss to the Vikings in Week 1 is any indication, this offense could be one-dimensional without its starting running back. Looking to rebound from a disappointing result, the Packers get a favorable matchup here, traveling to New York as 5.5-point favorites. Pretty good chance they’ll come through with 3 touchdowns, getting that first win. A lot of home run attempts from Jordan Love led to a very inefficient passing day against the Vikings, completing only 21 of 42 passes. The good news was that the strong play of the team’s receivers gave Love reason to attempt deep shots, leading to a massive day with 387 yards and 2 touchdowns. It remains to be seen if the Jets’ success defending the pass to open the season was real or simply a result of facing Cam Ward and the Titans. While they were effective at preventing big yardage against passers last season (226 per game), they also allowed a league-worst 36 passing touchdowns. Love’s protection will be under a microscope after he was sacked 4 times by the Vikings defense, and it becomes a bigger concern if the Packers are unable to run the ball again. Green Bay’s running game averaged only 3.1 yards on 21 attempts against Minnesota, with MarShawn Lloyd and Chris Brooks struggling to make it past the line of scrimmage. Lloyd was the lead ball carrier, as expected, with 13 carries to Brooks’ 7, but his 2.8 yards per attempt simply isn’t going to cut it and Brooks saw 8 more snaps. It wouldn’t be a shock to see Brooks get more looks if the trend continues early against the Jets, though this matchup looks much more favorable for all running backs involved. After allowing 140 rushing yards per game last season, 4th-worst in the league, the Jets allowed only 68 yards last week but still 4.9 yards per carry. The running game wasn’t happening in Week 1; this matchup should spark some improvement. We were reminded what a difference Christian Watson can make when he’s healthy, after one of the best games of his career against Minnesota (6 catches for 147 yards and 2 touchdowns). His electric deep speed shone against the Vikings, though the Jets pass defense limited big performances from wideouts last season with only 136 yards allowed per game. Matthew Golden also had a career day in receptions (6) and yards (95) in Week 1, though he wasn’t always on the same page with Jordan Love, catching only 6 of 12 targets."},{"t":"HOU","x":"The 4 touchdowns the Texans scored last week should be enough to win most games, especially with Houston having one of the best defenses. They were matched up with an elite Buffalo offense, though, and here’s another powerhouse group. Cincinnati actually underachieved in the opener (33 points, but with 4 field goals and 2 defensive touchdowns), but they’re a tough test for the Texans. Houston will likely need to again open things up, and it’s a promising matchup. Only one team allowed more yards and two more points than Cincinnati last year. His game ended with a sack-lost fumble, one of 3 sacks and 2 turnovers he was involved in, but did some nice stuff in C.J. Stroud the opener, too. Throwing for 274 yards and 2 TDs, while leading two other touchdown drives, should be enough to for Houston to win plenty of games. He’ll probably need to keep it up here, with Joe Burrow on the other side. The good thing is that the Bengals ranked 25th in pass defense a year ago, allowing 33 TDs (3rd-most), and too early to say they’re much better. (Baker Mayfield completed 23 of 28 last week; the team’s turnovers were the killer.) We’ll give Cincinnati some credit for improvement -they sacked Mayfield 4 times -- and note that Stroud has struggled with pass rush of late, not just Week 1 but throughout last year’s postseason. But he should have some open receivers this week. especially looks Nico Collins promising, catching 7 passes and a score on 10 targets last week. Injuries have left him arguably the team’s only viable wideout, but the Bills didn’t have much luck slowing him down and the Bengals probably won’t fare much better. Cincinnati allowed only 9 touchdowns to wide receivers last season, which is hard to understand, but 2 TDs in the opener (minimal success covering DJ Moore). Xavier Hutchinson played the 2nd-most snaps at the position in the opener, a little more than Kayshon Boutte, and out-targeted him 6-2. Results were modest (3 for 32), but just 1 reception for the newcomer. Jaylin Noel had a pair of receptions totaling 53 yards, including a big grab to put Houston in the red zone late, but those were his only targets while playing under a third of the snaps. Favorable matchup, but hard to make a case for any behind Collins at this point. Dalton Schultz, instead, was the beneficiary of those wide receiver injuries, seeing 8 targets (he averaged 6 a year ago). He"},{"t":"IND","x":"It was a disappointing Week 1 in Indianapolis. Baltimore rolled up 506 yards in a 41-23 win, outgaining the Colts by 255 yards. The follow-up doesn’t look much easier, a prime-time trip to Arrowhead against a Kansas City team that just held Denver to 176 yards. The Colts are 6.5-point underdogs with a total of 48.5, which works out to about 21 points. That’s a big step down from last season, when Indianapolis averaged 27.4 points (8th). Daniel Jones showed some rust in his return from last season’s Achilles injury, averaging only 5.4 yards per attempt despite chasing a big deficit for much of the game. He opened with a 65-yard touchdown drive, but the Colts’ next 5 possessions ended in punts or turnovers. Jones had some success throwing to his top 2 wideouts: that pair caught 10 of 12 targets, while Jones was just 9 of 19 throwing to everyone else. He also ran only once after averaging 3.6 attempts per game last season, perhaps a sign the team is hesitant to put him in harm’s way this early. Kansas City has a rebuilt secondary, but allowed only 19 points (6th) and 205 passing yards per game (8th) last season. In last year’s 23-20 overtime loss at Arrowhead, Jones threw for 181 yards and 2 touchdowns, but went just 8 of 18 for 83 yards in the second half. About 230 yards and a touchdown looks like fair expectation. The Colts are at their best when they’re able to feed Jonathan Taylor. He was productive against Baltimore, with 19 carries for 98 yards and 3 catches for 23 yards, right in line with his usage last season, but falling behind early kept Indianapolis from leaning on him the way it wanted. This is a tougher matchup. Kansas City had a top-10 run defense last season (106 yards per game, 9th), and they held Taylor to 58 rushing yards on 16 carries (plus 2 catches for 8 yards) in last year’s meeting. The scoring upside is still there, with Indianapolis running for 27 touchdowns last season (2nd), but a big yardage day likely depends on the Colts keeping it close. Taylor should play full-time at least; rookie backup Seth McGowan played only 6 snaps. It’s no shock that Alec Pierce and Keenan Allen appeared to be on snap counts after sitting out much of training camp. Pierce, coming off offseason ankle surgery, still led the team in receiving yards (61) and tied Allen for the team lead in targets (6). Allen played two-thirds of the snaps and Pierce 60 perce"},{"t":"JAX","x":"The Jaguars opened their follow-up to a 13-4 division-title season by embarrassing Cleveland, leading 24-0 at halftime. Week 2 is a much different assignment. They visit Denver as 2.5-point underdogs with a total of 43.5, putting Jacksonville’s implied total around 20.5 points; that’s a big drop for an offense that averaged 27.9 points (6th) a year ago. The Jaguars did win here in Week 16 last season, 34-20, scoring on 6 of 7 possessions at one point. But Denver was a top-3 defense in both yards and points allowed last year, and it will be motivated after getting pushed around on Monday night. Trevor Lawrence threw 4 touchdowns against the Browns, but this is a far tougher matchup. Denver allowed only 18 touchdown passes last season (tied for 4th-fewest), and 3 of those came from Lawrence himself (23 of 36 for 279 yards, no interceptions). Expect the Broncos to have some different looks ready after that performance. The pass rush is the bigger concern. Denver led the league with 68 sacks a year ago, while Jacksonville’s line kept Lawrence mostly clean in the opener (1 sack on 24 dropbacks). Mixed signals, but no chance this is as easy as Week 1. Around 230 yards and 1-2 touchdowns is more reasonable. The backfield is starting to shake out after Travis Etienne’s departure, with Bhayshul Tuten at the head of the table. He out-snapped Chris Rodriguez 28-23 and out-touched him 16-6, running 15 times for 66 yards and catching a pass for 22 more. Rodriguez’s role was bigger than his 6 carries for 23 yards suggest. He rotated with Tuten on the opening drive, got the only goal-line snap, and was on the field for more of the play-action looks out of heavier packages. Ameer Abdullah handled some third-down and garbage-time work (4 carries for 32 yards, 1 catch). It isn’t a favorable matchup for anyone here, though. The Broncos allowed 91 rushing yards per game last season (2nd) and just 11 rushing touchdowns (4th-fewest). They were thrashed by Kansas City in the opener, however, and are on a short week, so we’re thinking Jacksonville might fare a little better than that. The offseason hype surrounding Parker Washington held up in Week 1. He led Jacksonville’s wideouts in targets (6), catches (5), and yards (83), and his 30yard diving touchdown opened the scoring. He likely would have done more if the Jaguars hadn’t built such a big lead so quickly. Jakobi Meyers and B"},{"t":"KC","x":"After Week 1, it looks like a new day for the Kansas City offense. Andy Reid has long been known for aggressive play-calling, but with a banged-up offensive line missing left tackle Josh Simmons, Kansas City put up 31 points by running the ball 38 times against a Denver defense that allowed only 18 points per game last season (3rd). That’s a sharp turn from 2025, when Kansas City ranked 25th in rushing and 21st in scoring on the way to a 6-11 finish. They’re 6.5-point favorites this week against a Colts defense that allowed 41 points and 506 yards to Baltimore, and an implied total of 27.5 points looks very reachable. Patrick Mahomes didn’t play in the preseason while recovering from last December’s torn ACL, and frankly, it showed. He completed only 15 of 27 passes for 184 yards, with 2 touchdowns and an interception, the pick coming on a clear third-quarter miscommunication with Xavier Worthy. The knee looked 100 percent, though. Wearing a brace, he ran 7 times for 23 yards and scored the game’s first touchdown on the ground. The matchup is excellent. Indianapolis had the 2nd-worst pass defense in the league last season (262 yards per game), and Lamar Jackson just threw for 324 yards against them with an 82.8 QBR. Mahomes has generally put up big numbers against Colts defensive coordinator Lou Anarumo, including a season-high 352 yards (29 of 46) in last year’s overtime win. Some of his Week 1 struggles were a credit to Denver, which led the league with 68 sacks last season. The question is whether his timing with the receivers comes around on a short week, but above-average numbers look likely. The Kenneth Walker era began in style. The Super Bowl LX MVP picked up where he left off in Seattle, running 23 times for 173 yards and a touchdown and adding 3 catches for 18 yards and a receiving score. That came against a Denver defense that allowed just 91 rushing yards per game last season (2nd). For much of the game Walker was the only offense Kansas City could muster, with his ability to cut, slash, and manufacture holes giving the team a dimension they haven’t had this decade. He’s unlikely to see the same workload every week, but with Kansas City favored by nearly a touchdown, another heavy dose is likely. The Colts allowed 102 rushing yards per game last season (7th), but Derrick Henry and the Ravens blew that average out of the water last week, rushing f"},{"t":"LV","x":"Las Vegas won in Week 1 for the first time since 2023, with a relatively easy time against the rebuilding Dolphins. They haven’t convinced Vegas yet, entering Week 2 as 7-point underdogs as they visit the AFC West rival Chargers, who suffered an embarrassing home loss to the Cardinals to open their season. It’s a new year, but the Raiders totaled 23 points in last year’s series, and might not have the firepower to be dramatically better here. Kirk Cousins did enough to get the win last week, even though he threw 2 interceptions and averaged only 5.3 yards per attempt. The veteran still threw for 160 yards and 3 touchdowns, playing it safe as a game manager while the Raiders held the lead for most of the contest. With a lacking wide receiver room and Brock Bowers sidelined, 13 of his 21 completions went to running backs and tight ends. The loss of defensive coordinator Jesse Minter, now Baltimore’s head coach, showed for a Chargers defense that allowed 277 passing yards and sacked Jacoby Brissett only once, but last year’s dominance shows the upside with a Los Angeles defense that allowed an elite 195 passing yards per game and ranked 10th with 45 sacks. It’s unlikely Cousins will have quite as much success against this group. Ashton Jeanty showed little sign of the sprained ankle he suffered in late August. He dominated Klint Kubiak’s offensive gameplan with 29 touches, leading the Raiders in both rushing and receiving, to finish with 147 yards and 2 scores while seeing 74 percent of the snaps. The Chargers represent a far more difficult opponent in spite of what we saw last week, as they still held Arizona to only 3.4 yards per carry and 116 yards after averaging 105 yards allowed last season (8thbest). The fact that Arizona running backs combined for 4 catches and 18 yards is also notable, continuing the Chargers’ 2025 trend of limiting running backs to only 3.5 catches and 25 yards per contest. Jeanty is likely to get a heavy workload again out of necessity, but all signs point to a much tougher showing. He also struggled against Los Angeles last year, totaling only 105 yards in two games. Rookie Mike Washington backed up the significant preseason hype with 7 rushes for 41 yards against Miami, but his role could be mitigated if the Raiders get behind early. Fair-weather NFL fans are unlikely to be able to name a current Raiders wide receiver, and fantasy"},{"t":"LAC","x":"The Chargers’ 2026 campaign wasn’t supposed to start like it did in Week 1, getting punched in the mouth as 9.5-point home favorites against the Cardinals. They go back to the drawing board against a familiar division foe in the Raiders, who they’ve beaten four times in a row, each win by at least 11 points. Los Angeles is a 7-point home favorite with a total of 45.5. Justin Herbert had an inefficient opening day against the Cardinals, completing 17 of 27 passes for 209 yards with one touchdown and one interception, below last year’s 66.4 percent completion rate and 233 yards per game. He threw a pair of touchdowns in both matchups against the Raiders defense last season, but the Chargers’ bangedup offensive line needs to do a better job against the Vegas pass rush after Herbert was sacked 3 times by Arizona in Week 1. The Raiders defense allowed only 214 passing yards per game last season (13th best), and its pass rush wreaked havoc on Malik Willis last week, sacking him 5 times with one pick. Herbert’s 5 rushes for 29 yards in Week 1 is worth noting after the Raiders struggled to keep Willis in the pocket, with the mobile quarterback finishing with 6 carries for 36 yards. The possible absence of Ladd McConkey (ribs) is a major storyline, potentially limiting Herbert’s passing ceiling. Trailing for much of the game against Arizona, the plan of leaning on Omarion Hampton and the running game was disrupted. He had only 12 carries for 43 yards and a touchdown, and failed to tally any stats as a receiver, seeing only a 58 percent snap rate. Meanwhile, change of pace back Keaton Mitchell overcame a questionable injury tag to rush 4 times for 9 yards. Offensive coordinator Mike McDaniel’s reputation as a run schemer will likely come into play more if and when the Chargers take a lead against the Raiders this week, though Las Vegas managed to hold De’Von Achane to only 36 yards on 11 carries. Hampton played in only one of the two games against the Raiders last season, totaling only 25 yards on 9 touches in his worst showing of the season, and the Raiders did an excellent job limiting opposing offenses to only 117 yards rushing last season. That said, the Chargers ran through the Raiders late in the year for 192 yards, with Hampton sidelined, so a bounce-back game could be on tap. Ladd McConkey’s status will be a major question mark after he left with a rib injury"},{"t":"LAR","x":"Rough start for the Rams, appearing jetlagged in an ugly loss to San Francisco for one of their nine “home” games. They’ll have a little extra time to get ready here, and the matchup seems favorable. The Giants had a bottom-10 defense a year ago (yards and points allowed). They seem improved, but one game is not enough to say they’ll be shutting down the Rams. The betting public still likes them: They’re touchdown favorites, expected to score 27-28 points in this Monday Night affair. Just one good series for the defending MVP last week, with Matthew Stafford turning a lousy game. He’s old and so is one of his main weapons, but a little too soon to start saying it’s over. The 49ers are a defense with a lot of familiarity against him, and it seemed like the travel affected LA’s offense. Things should go better here. The Giants were below-average against the pass a year ago, and would have been worse if they weren’t next-to-last against the run. They have some pass rushers, but didn’t sack Dak Prescott last week and Stafford’s line knows Job 1 is keeping him upright. There’s reason for caution with the offense after that Week 1 dud, but feeling is he’ll bounce back with above-average numbers. Stafford was stopped on a goal-line sneak last week that would have made his box score look a little better, but the Rams probably won’t try many more of those plays. (Even that one was surprising, with Stafford’s last TD on the ground occurring in 2022.) Kyren Williams and Blake Corum were a one-two punch in the opener, each with 10-11 carries, with Corum a little more effective (5.4 per attempt versus 3.7). It should be noted that Williams got all three of the pair’s targets in the passing game and also the goal-line work, as was the case late last season. It’s a committee, but Williams is still dominating the higher-value opportunities. As for this week, the Giants had a lousy run defense a year ago. It looks improved, holding Javonte Williams to 3.4 yards per attempt last week, but the Rams run the ball the better, plus Williams wound up getting in the end zone twice. Maybe not a good enough matchup to consider Corum, but Williams looks fine. Puka Nacua had LA’s best offensive play in the opener, making a contested catch and then shaking a couple of tackles on a 41-yard gain to set their only touchdown. A preseason injury didn’t appear to be bothering him. This defense"},{"t":"MIA","x":"If losing by 2 touchdowns to the Raiders doesn’t hint at what’s in store for the Dolphins this season, this matchup probably will. San Francisco is favored by 12.5, with the over-under of 45.5 suggesting Miami will finish with about 16-17 points. Maybe the 49ers will still be shaking off the effects of spending a week in Australia. That’s the best argument for the Dolphins making a game of things here. There are going to be some rough weeks ahead for De’Von Achane. Teams will stack the line to make Miami’s inexperienced quarterback take on more of the offense, and the Dolphins will be playing from behind most games. There’s little doubt the offense will be one of the league’s poorer groups in terms of rushing yards and touchdowns, and the quarterback is going to account for a higher percentage of that production than with most teams. Achane’s a talent and many weeks he’ll be probably be successful doing it on his own, but others he’ll be spinning his wheels. This defense can definitely hold him to a poor day running it. It ranked 11th a year ago, and that was while dealing with some significant injuries; it’s better now. The Rams’ duo averaged 4.5 per attempt in the opener, but that’s a high-powered offense that also had a top-level passing game to contend with; Miami isn’t either of those things. Hope for plenty of receiving work (Achane caught 4 for 30 last week); that’s how he’ll pay off in a lot of games, while maybe busting a long run on occasion. The 49ers frustrated Matthew Stafford for most of the opener, so best to steer clear of Malik Willis. He threw for 220 at Las Vegas, but completed just 55 percent and also took 5 sacks. Willis is an active runner, going for 39 yards and a 14-yard touchdown in that one. That production will give him some usable weeks, especially when there’s a lesser defense on the schedule. If the game goes the way the point spread suggests, there will be some late production to be had. But about 210 passing yards and a couple of total touchdowns seems the best to hope for, and San Francisco should be ready for him as a scrambler. Highlight of the opener was probably Caleb Douglas catching 5 passes for 94 yards on 7 targets. Douglas drew praise all preseason and his debut lived up to it. Maybe Chris Bell eventually emerges as the better player, but Bell has missed a lot of time working his way back from a torn ACL and the team"},{"t":"MIN","x":"The Vikings stole the opener from Green Bay (they did the same to this opponent in Week 1 a year ago). It seems like a favorable matchup for their offense, with the Bears having a bottom-10 defense (both yards and points) a year ago. But they’ll be starting their backup quarterback, facing an opportunistic defense (and high-scoring offense). Probably an average to slightly below-average matchup. Kyler Murray suffered a scary concussion in the opener, making him unlikely to clear the protocol for this game. We’re proceeding as if it will be Carson Wentz, and will revise on Friday if necessary. Wentz did well to pull out last week’s win with some late-game magic, throwing 3 touchdowns and not turning it over. But he threw for just 133 yards, with multiple scoring drives beginning deep in Green Bay territory; a long kick return and a couple of turnovers were key. This is not to dismiss Wentz, but the initial plan was for him to be the No. 3 this it season. It’s a quarterback friendly system, Wentz has some arm talent, and he’s facing a leaky pass defense (22nd last year, plus Bryce Young threw for 361 and 3 TDs). But we’re recommending caution, and Wentz was characteristically up and down in last year’s 5 starts for Minnesota. Twice he threw for 300-plus yards, while in the other three he averaged 184. He threw 6 TDs and 5 interceptions in those contests, while taking nearly 4 sacks per game (19 total) and fumbling 3 times. He was sacked 3 times while attempting only 19 passes last week. Kevin O’Connell will probably be trying to play things fairly close to the vest. Chicago was leaky against the run last year (27th) and looked no better in the opener, with Carolina running for 125 yards and 2 TDs. Minnesota backs didn’t look great against Green Bay, with both Aaron Jones and Jordan Mason averaging under 4 yards per attempt, but the Vikings stuck with it anyway, with those backs combining for 27 carries and each running in a short touchdown. It’s a tandem, in which generally Mason should get more chances around the goal line and Jones will do more in the passing game. The run and short passes should be a point of emphasis in the offense, giving both a chance of helping a lineup. Early in the week Mason was dealing with a sore thumb from the opener, so his practice participation will need to be confirmed; for now, the assumption is he’s fine (but Jones would get"},{"t":"NE","x":"Rough start for the Patriots, although a road game against the defending champs was a tall order -- and only a couple of late mistakes kept them from springing the upset. Now they’re home against a defense that isn’t quite as good. The Steelers were 26th in yards allowed a year ago and below-average in scoring defense, and shutting down the Cooper Rush Falcons last week doesn’t mean very much. These teams met in Week 3 a year ago, a 21-14 Steelers win, but the Patriots did roll up 387 yards of total offense that day. Drake Maye looked good for a lot of Week 1, but then the fourth quarter happened (3 interceptions). No sugarcoating his costly mistakes, but he made some good throws before them, and -- most encouragingly from a fantasy perspective -- he ran 7 times for 47 yards. Here he faces a defense that was at the opposite end of the spectrum against the pass last year than Seattle’s, ranking 30th, allowing 261 yards per game (30th) and 30 touchdown passes. Better matchup for passing, even if it’s fair to speculate they’ll go a little more conservative with the playcalling this week. Maye is clearly going to be an active runner, helping him show up with a ranking more favorable than his passing alone -- especially already down his top wideout -- would suggest. Maye threw for 268 and 2 TDs while running for 45 more in last year’s game. The conventional ground game struggled in last year’s game, with Rhamondre Stevenson and TreVeyon Henderson combining to carry 15 times for 46 yards. It was more of the same for Stevenson at Seattle (3rd in run defense a year ago) last week, with 18 carries for 51 yards (2.8 per attempt). Pittsburgh was 13th last season and while Bijan Robinson ran for 83 yards last week, he averaged just 4.0 per attempt. So set expectations low for the ground game, which may again be mostly Stevenson, with Corey Kiner a change of pace. Henderson missed Week 1 with an ankle injury and it’s uncertain whether he’ll return for this one; even if he does, it would be in a secondary role. Where Stevenson can best help fantasy franchises is in the passing game. He caught 5 passes for 44 yards at Seattle, and those plays should be part of the gameplan, especially considering the way Maye’s downfield throws went last week. Bijan caught 8 passes for 90 yards and a score last week (granted, he’s Bijan Robinson). A.J. Brown (ankle, IR) will miss at least"},{"t":"NO","x":"The Saints came up just short in overtime at Detroit, and now face another situation that will be tough to come away with a win. But Baltimore’s defense hasn’t been scary for a while, finishing in the bottom 10 in yards and below-average in points allowed last season, then giving up 3 touchdowns to the Colts last week. Seems like at least an average matchup, even though it’s probably another high-scoring loss. Baltimore ranked 29th in pass defense a year ago, and holding Daniel Jones under 200 yards in his first game action since suffering a torn Achilles late last year isn’t notable. Seems like an OK situation for Tyler Shough, who was just 10 of 22 for 85 yards in the first half at Detroit -- and 25 of 34 for 325 yards and 3 TDs after that. It’s a good offensive system, he’s got some capable receivers, and the team might not have a lot of success running the ball in some games (including this one). Not that he’ll be attempting 56 passes again, but it’s another week New Orleans may need to lean on the pass. Shough will typically stay in the pocket, but he did average 17 rushing yards a year ago, with 3 TDs while starting nine contests, so it’s part of his game. Travis Etienne also started out slow at Detroit, with 9 total yards in the first half, half as many as backup Kendre Miller. Things opened up for Etienne later (10 for 46 on the day), plus he caught 7 passes for 32 yards. Alvin Kamara will probably be active this week to take some of those chances, but should still be Etienne getting most of the touches. This is a tough matchup, with Baltimore 10th in run defense a year ago. Jonathan Taylor went for 98 yards and 2 TDs last week, but he’s one of the game’s best backs. The chance of New Orleans again being forced into catchup mode makes it seem like Etienne’s work as a receiver will again be key to his value. The team is easing Kamara back in (making him inactive even after he practiced fully last week), so probably a minimal role for the veteran if he’s available. Chris Olave and Devaughn Vele make for a nice duo. Olave’s the former first-round pick with the high ceiling, putting up career-best numbers last year one year after concussions seemed to be derailing his career. Detroit had no answer for him last week (10 for 182, most after halftime). Vele’s the former seventh-rounder who caught 19 balls in his last four games a year ago and 7 for 69 and"},{"t":"NYG","x":"This game looks a little more promising than a week ago. LA’s offense might have some issues, but more importantly its defense won’t have one or both of its big offseason additions. Still a tough matchup for the Giants, but their offense might be just fine. Betting lines have the Giants scoring 20-21 points. Myles Garrett is on IR and Aaron Donald is still working to get into game shape, making this matchup a lot more palatable for Jaxson Dart. Dart had one of his cleanest games in the opener, throwing for 230 yards and 3 TDs while running for 54 more. No turnovers (he had 5 interceptions and 5 fumbles in 12 starts last year) and only 2 sacks (about 3 per game last season). This is a much bigger challenge, on the road against a Rams team in a foul mood and with some extra rest after an embarrassing loss in Australia. But LA will again be shorthanded defensively, and they got carved up pretty good by Brock Purdy. Just one game, but it doesn’t look like a defense to be feared at the moment. Last year the Rams were just 20th in pass defense, so there are some coverage issues, and Dart’s willingness to run it makes him look even better. The Rams were 12th in run defense a year ago, but that defense didn’t show up in Australia, with San Francisco backs running for 133 yards and 5.5 per attempt. Cam Skattebo averaged 4.5 per attempt versus Dallas (18 for 81), looking fully healthy after last year’s leg injury and doubtless helped by his running quarterback. The Rams have had some extra time to prepare and should offer more resistance than Dallas (a bottom-10 run defense a year ago), but Skattebo will be the leader runner and he should generally be involved as a receiver (no targets last week, but 3 receptions per game a year ago). As for the No. 2, that’s harder to lock in on. Najee Harris was inactive last week while Tyrone Tracy lost a fumble on one of his only 2 snaps, enabling Devin Singletary to step into the job (38 yards on 6 runs and 4 receptions). Tracy definitely isn’t safe to use, but not a given that Singletary will get those chances; Harris was signed in August with a promise of a meaningful role. Skattebo’s the only back we’ll recommend at the moment. Considering he wasn’t even certain to play until shortly before the game, Malik Nabers had a solid outing (6 for 69 on a team-high 9 targets). Assuming no setback he should be locked in as a go-to going"},{"t":"NYJ","x":"There’s more hope among Jets faithful this week than there has been in a long time after their impressive 23-10 win at Tennessee. If only for one week, Geno Smith looks like more than just a placeholder quarterback and Aaron Glenn looks like the right man for the job. That said, they come home as 5.5-point underdogs to a Packers squad that’s in a foul mood after a bad road loss. No more than a couple of touchdowns and 20 points looks like their ceiling this week. Geno Smith showed glimpses of his better self with a highly efficient Week 1 game against the Titans. The veteran completed 19 of 24 passes for 215 yards, serving as the game manager the team hoped for when they signed him. Of course, a high completion rate hasn’t been the issue for Smith recently, as he completed more than 67 percent of his passes in each of the last two seasons. Negative plays have been the problem, and against the Titans he didn’t take a sack or throw a pick. The Packers allowed only 151 passing yards to Minnesota with backup Carson Wentz called into action and are coming off a season in which they were in the top 10 with 207 passing yards allowed per game, but much of that performance was with the now-injured Micah Parsons on the field. We didn’t see enough evidence from Smith in Week 1 to consider him born again in fantasy, but the Packers defense isn’t a sure thing to harass him into a poor day. The debut of offensive coordinator Frank Reich and an early lead brought 39 rush attempts in Week 1, music to the ears of those rostering Breece Hall and Braelon Allen. The running back pair did a lot with the work, totaling 142 yards rushing, with Hall outpacing Allen’s attempts by better than 2-to-1 (22 to 10). Still, Allen’s production, particularly on passing downs, is encouraging for his potential behind Hall; he also had a long touchdown negated by a penalty. Green Bay’s defense did manage to hold the Vikings to only 107 yards rushing (3.1 yards per carry), after finishing in the middle of the pack with 118 rushing yards allowed per game last season, so call it a slightly below-average matchup. Garrett Wilson’s rebound campaign after playing only seven games last season started on a strong note with 6 catches for 79 yards, an improvement on his previous career average of 63 yards per game. He looked fully healthy after being shut down with a knee injury last season, and already l"},{"t":"PHI","x":"The Eagles opened their 2026 campaign with a win against Washington by the skin of their teeth, and have reason to expect an easier time in Week 2 as 7-point favorites at Tennessee, up from 5.5 at the open. The Titans were embarrassed by the Jets at home and, at least on the surface, don’t stack up well with Cam Ward’s continued struggles and a defense that’s finding its footing under a new coaching staff. Jalen Hurts could have been more accurate to start the OC Sean Mannion era, completing only 14 of 25 passes, but the overall production was favorable against the Commanders defense after he threw 3 touchdowns. Going on the road in Week 2 brings its own set of challenges, but the Titans defense hasn’t shown any signs of improvement so far. After allowing 248 passing yards per game last season, they looked leaky against Geno Smith last week (215 yards and 9.0 yards per attempt). Hurts’ Week 1 yardage (203) was on par with last year’s average of 202, and he killed the Commanders on the ground with 46 rushing yards, 20 more than last year’s average. After Tennessee generated almost no pass rush in Week 1, it’s fair to predict that Hurts will have his way with another defense this week. Saquon Barkley didn’t have the best game for fantasy managers to open his season, with only 80 total yards, but he looked just fine at 5.5 yards per carry on 15 rushes against Washington’s defense. Tennessee allowed 115 yards per game on the ground last season, but the Jets ran through the Titans for 152 yards and two touchdowns. Tennessee held running backs to only 3.4 catches per game last season, tied for 3rd-best in the league, and allowed only 2 catches to them last week. With the possibility of a big lead this week, Barkley is likely to be busier in Week 2 after seeing only 16 touches to start the season. Backup Tank Bigsby had only one carry on Sunday, but would certainly figure to see more work if the Eagles get out to a big lead. Tennessee allowed three passing gains of 30-plus yards last week, which is music to the ears of DeVonta Smith and Dontayvion Wicks. The pair beat Washington’s defense with huge plays in Week 1, including a 64-yard gain from Wicks and a 31-yard gainer down the sideline from Smith. The Titans defense allowed a massive 166 yards per game to wide receivers last season (4th-most), and both Garrett Wilson (6 catches, 79 yards) and Adonai Mitchell (3"},{"t":"PIT","x":"The offense sputtered for much of the Atlanta game, disappointing considering the pass rushers the Falcons didn’t have available. Perhaps the thinking was the only way they could lose to a team starting its third-string quarterback was making a bunch of careless mistakes. They’ll need to do more here, facing a Patriots team whose offense was similarly poor in the opener -- but in a much tougher matchup, at Seattle. Pittsburgh won ugly here early last season, managing only 203 total yards in a 21-14 win. Aaron Rodgers threw for just 139 yards in this matchup a year ago. He threw for 221 last week, but it took 40 attempts to get there, which shouldn’t be the usual offensive approach. New England had a top-10 pass defense last season and might not be much different, so it’s not a great situation, and Rodgers himself may be feeling his 42 years. This was a lesser pass rush last season (35 sacks), but looks better after adding Dre’Mont Jones and second-rounder Gabe Jacas, who each had a sack at Seattle. Atlanta got him twice last week, which Pittsburgh won’t want to be a regular occurrence; the ball should be coming out quick. Rodgers did throw 2 TDs in last year’s meeting, but unlikely he has a big game throwing it. New England ranked 6th in run defense a year ago; too soon to say if they’re that good now. Jadarian Price averaged 5.2 yards per attempt, though Seattle’s other backs were bottled up. Jaylen Warren went nowhere in last year’s game (18 carries for 47 yards, which is 2.6 per attempt); he ran well against Atlanta (10 for 46). It’s a tandem with Rico Dowdle, but Dowdle fared poorly in the opener, with his 8 carries going for just 15 yards. But he played a little more, and has run well enough in the past (both Dallas and Carolina) to think they’ll get him going. Warren was also more productive in the passing game (5 for 27, on one more target than Dowdle, who went just 2 for 6). Warren should get more of those chances most weeks, and those plays which get the ball out of Rodgers’ hands will likely be a big part of the game plan. Next two games are against the Bengals and Browns, which look like better spots to get the ground game ramped up. Targets shouldn’t be a problem for DK Metcalf. He had 10 in Week 1; no one else had more than 6. Only 4 were completed, but there were a couple of deep shots in there could have been completed, one of which should hav"},{"t":"SF","x":"Beating the Rams last week was considered an upset; winning big here will not be. The Dolphins lost by two scores last week to a team that won 3 games a year ago, and the point spread of 13.5 is at least 5 points more than every other game this week. Should be a laugher, with the 49ers as likely as any team to be scoring in the 30s this week. Christian McCaffrey didn’t get in the end zone in Week 1 and finished with 4 fewer carries than rookie Kaelon Black, but it’s not quite a committee backfield. McCaffrey was reportedly dealing with cramping during the game that affected distribution of carries, and McCaffrey remains the back of choice around the end zone and in the passing game (an 8-1 edge in targets). Black is going to play more than McCaffrey’s previous backups. He looks good, and the starter’s workload needs to be dialed back after a career-high 413 touches and one of his least efficient seasons running it (3.9 yards per attempt). But McCaffrey will still be accounting for most of the chances (including the most fantasy-relevant ones) and the matchup looks great. Miami ranked 26th in run defense a year ago, shed some talent in the offseason, and just let Ashton Jeanty put up 147 total yards and 2 TDs just a couple of weeks after some were counting him out for the season. Great week to use McCaffrey, and considering how the game should go, Black has some value, too. Brock Purdy threw a bad interception early against the Rams, but he was near-perfect after that. He threw 3 touchdowns and added an additional 29 yard on the ground to his 205 passing against one of the initial Super Bowl favorites. At home against the Dolphins looks a little more favorable. Miami ranked outside the top 20 (both yards and points allowed) a year ago, middle-of-the-pack for sacks and takeaways. The Raiders threw 3 touchdowns against them last week. Good spot for Purdy to come through with above-average numbers in what’s been a passfirst offense for a while now. We’d be higher on him if it didn’t seem pretty likely a lot of the offense will be via the run. De’Zhaun Stribling looked like he was going to be a big part of the offense in the opener, but that’s gone; he’ll miss at least the next month with an ankle sprain. Demarcus Robinson was the beneficiary, joining Mike Evans and Deebo Samuel in the top 3, giving the 49ers the league’s oldest trio by a wide margin. It’s fair t"},{"t":"SEA","x":"Defense helped Seattle get by the Patriots, even though they lost their starting quarterback for possibly the next month. While their offense didn’t do much, due both to a tough matchup and a backup quarterback in the lineup, this game should help with one of those concerns. The Cardinals surprised a disjointed Chargers team last week, but this is still the defense that ranked in the bottom 10 against the run, the pass and in yards and points allowed a year ago. The Seahawks themselves scored 67 points in the two games. Drew Lock will start, with Sam Darnold (hip), possibly missing the next 4-6 weeks. (There have been conflicting timelines, but that’s the most common estimate.) He did well to pull out the Week 1 win, but expectations should be very modest. He’s started five games the past two seasons (for the Giants) and reached 230 passing yards once and threw more than 1 touchdown once. He’ll be asked to hand the ball off and complete some short passes along the way, not operate a high-level passing game. The 187 yards and 1 TD against the Patriots -- with his wideout doing the heavy lifting on a 45-yard touchdown -- is a fair starting point. He’s facing a lesser defense (Arizona was bottom-10 against the pass a year ago, allowing 31 touchdowns), which helps. Lock will run a little (50-plus yards in a pair of those Giants starts), but they’d probably prefer he stay in one piece (2 carries for 13 yards last week), particularly with nextin-line Jalen Milroe having shown very little to this point. It’s a committee backfield, with Jadarian Price and George Holani splitting time. That was the case last week, at least, with Price starting but Holani having only 2 fewer carries and playing in passing situations, while Emanuel Wilson and AJ Barner got short-yardage chances. Seattle did this most of last year with departed Kenneth Walker and injured Zach Charbonnet. Price looked really explosive with his 10 carries, going for 52 yards; Holani’s 8 attempts went for 29 yards, while Wilson played only 3 snaps. Price also caught his 2 targets for 6 yards, so the division of labor could shift going forward. But not a featured back at the moment. This is a favorable matchup, with the Cardinals allowing 127 rushing yards per game a year ago, with 19 touchdowns. They were better in Week 1, but the Chargers got away from the run after falling behind. Seattle, with a few ext"},{"t":"TB","x":"Tampa Bay’s season started with a disappointing 33-27 road loss in Cincinnati. The Bucs can look at the opener as glass half empty in that they lost 4 fumbles and were outgained on the road 351-282. On the other hand, they lost by only one score and have a soft landing in their home opener as 8.5-point favorites over the already-struggling Browns, in a game with the lowest total on the board (40.5, down from 42.5 at the open). Bucs should be scoring in the mid-tohigh 20s this week. Baker Mayfield’s year opened with three lost fumbles, plus he was sacked four times by the Bengals defense, with 8 of his 23 completions going to running backs. Tampa Bay’s offensive line hopes to protect him better at home, and the quarterback was otherwise efficient, completing 23 of 28 passes for 216 yards and adding 30 yards and a score on the ground. The fears that Cleveland’s defense would falter after trading Myles Garrett were realized in Week 1, as Trevor Lawrence threw for 245 yards and four touchdowns while the Browns managed only one sack, a big letdown after they finished 3rd in the league with 53 last season. Giving Mayfield more time will be key, and there’s reason to think the Bucs will get it after the decline Cleveland’s pass rush showed. While it’s by no means a perfect matchup, Week 2 looks like a strong chance at a rebound for Mayfield. The aforementioned involvement in the passing game for Tampa Bay’s running backs, Bucky Irving and Kenny Gainwell, helped what was otherwise a forgettable day for the runners. Irving was ahead in the battle for playing time (35 snaps to 26) and also far more effective, with 8 carries for 45 yards and a score along with 7 catches for 48 yards. Gainwell’s role was more reminiscent of his history as a backup in Philadelphia and the first half of last year in Pittsburgh, seeing only 6 touches. With a game script that’s more likely to be on the plus side as a heavily favored home team, Gainwell should still have a role against the Browns, but Irving is a go-to for now. The Browns defense did a fair job holding Jacksonville’s running game in check in Week 1, all things considered, allowing 126 yards but only 3.9 yards per carry, after holding offenses to 116 rushing yards per game last season (16th). Emeka Egbuka led the Bucs with 63 yards on 5 catches against the Bengals, showing limited ill effects from a toe injury suffered last m"},{"t":"TEN","x":"The start of the Robert Saleh era was suboptimal, with the Titans offense looking ineffective in a 2310 home loss to the Jets. The test looks dramatically tougher in Week 2 against an Eagles team favored by a touchdown, coming off a 2422 victory over the Commanders. Unlikely the offense shows much (if any) improvement this week. Cam Ward is already showing some of his rookie struggles in Year 2. New OC Brian Daboll was brought in partly to fix his issues throwing the ball down the field, but Ward averaged a pitiful 4.4 yards per attempt in Week 1, completing 19 of 32 passes for 140 yards and a touchdown against the Jets and adding 21 yards on the ground. The Eagles allowed only 205 passing yards per game last season (6th best), and held Jayden Daniels in check during Week 1. If there’s any solace for Ward, Philadelphia’s defense allowed the second-most rushing yards to quarterbacks last season, and Ward’s production last week more than doubled his 9.4 rushing yards per game as a rookie. Overall, it’s a tough matchup for Ward as he still tries to get comfortable in Daboll’s offense. The surprising early deficit made Daboll more aggressive in the play calling in Week 1, to the detriment of Tony Pollard and Tyjae Spears. The duo combined for only 10 rush attempts against the Jets, and a spread of a touchdown against the Eagles doesn’t look conducive to a big rushing day in Week 2, either. The running game was one way to beat Vic Fangio’s defense last season, which allowed 124 yards per game and 20 touchdowns, and the Commanders also had success in Week 1 with 132 yards on the ground. Philadelphia allowed the 7th-most receiving yards in the league to running backs last season, an area where Pollard is capable despite catching only one ball in the opener. It’d be a sneaky decent matchup for Pollard if the Titans can keep this a game into the second half (they probably can’t). Ward’s quiet day obviously affected Tennessee’s wideouts in Week 1, with no receiver gaining more than 38 yards. Carnell Tate and Wan’Dale Robinson tied with 6 targets each, while Elic Ayomanor and Chimere Dike each caught a pass. Calvin Ridley never got going, failing to garner a target and seeing only 32 snaps. The lack of accuracy from Ward continues to be a red flag that will likely suppress the values of the wide receivers going forward, and Philadelphia’s excellent pass defense is furt"},{"t":"WAS","x":"The offense struggled last week, but that’s a really good Philadelphia defense. Dallas has a new coordinator and some new players, but if it’s much improved from last year’s bottom-3 trainwreck, it wasn’t apparent in the opener. Washington scored 22-23 points while losing both games a year ago, but should be a little better -- definitely healthier -- on offense now. The over-under of 50.5 is one of the two highest of the week. Jayden Daniels struggled for much of last week’s game, with the Eagles generally able to keep him in the pocket and force some errant throws. That’s one of the league’s best defenses, and while the Cowboys may be better than the punching bag of the past couple of seasons, they’ll need to prove it before anyone shies away from them. Even while struggling Daniels threw a couple of touchdowns and ran for 31 yards, hinting at what’s possible against a lesser defense in a game that should feature plenty of offense from both sides. Daniels was knocked out of the game at Dallas a year ago, but put up 191 total yards and 2 TDs in about a half worth of action before that happened. Dallas was also bottom-10 in run defense a year ago, and let the Giants run for 165 and a score last week. Some of that was their quarterback, but Washington has one of those threats too, making it more difficult for opponents to focus on the tailback. Jacory Croskey-Merritt is a modest talent, a seventh-rounder who got pushed aside by Chris Rodriguez a year ago. But he pounded out 63 yards and a score at Philadelphia, and this is a more favorable situation. Croskey-Merritt also ran for 105 yards and 2 TDs against this defense in Week 17 a year ago. Concern with the starter is the presence of Rachaad White, who played only 5 fewer snaps in Week 1. White will get some changeof-pace carries (7 in the opener, compared to 16 for Croskey-Merritt) and handle passing situations. Just 2 catches at Philadelphia, but probably more most weeks, particularly in games that figure to be higher-scoring, like this one. Croskey-Merritt caught 9 passes all of last season and his only chance in Week 1 lost 7 yards. But this looks the kind of matchup where both have value. Giants running backs had rushing and receiving scores last week. Stefon Diggs and Terry McLaurin are older wideouts, so it seems notable that third-rounder Antonio Williams was the Week 1 standout, catching all 4 passes"}];
